*** Spread of {{IPA|/k/}} > {{IPA|/k͡x/}} is geographically very limited and is not reflected in Modern Standard German.
** {{IPA|/b/}}, {{IPA|/d/}} and {{IPA|/ɡ/}} are devoiced.
*** In what ultimately gave rise to Standard German, this applies to {{IPA|/d/}} in all positions but to {{IPA|/b/}} and {{IPA|/ɡ/}} only when they are geminated. PwGmc *{{wikt-lang|gmw-x-proto|bruggju}} > ''brucca'' > ''Brücke'', but *{{wikt-lang|gmw-x-proto|leugan}} > ''liogan'' > ''lügen''.
* *''ē{{sub|2}}'' and *''ō'' are diphthongized into {{IPA|/ie/}} and {{IPA|/uo/}}, respectively.
* Proto-Germanic *''ai'' became ''ei'' except before {{IPA|/r/}}, {{IPA|/h/}}, {{IPA|/w/}} and word-finally, when it monophthongizes into ''ē'', which is also the reflex of unstressed *''ai''.
** Similarly, *''au'' > ''ō'' before {{IPA|/r/}}, {{IPA|/h/}} and all dentals; otherwise, *''au'' > ''ou''. PwGmc *''dauþu'' > OHG ''{{wikt-lang|goh|tōd}}'', but *''haubud'' > ''{{wikt-lang|goh|houbit}}''.
*** {{IPA|/h/}} refers there only to inherited {{IPA|/h/}} from PIE *''k'', not to the result of the consonant shift {{IPA|/x/}}, which is sometimes written as {{angbr|h}}.
* {{IPA|/eu/}} merges with {{IPA|/iu/}} under ''i''-umlaut and ''u''-umlaut but elsewhere is {{IPA|/io/}} (earlier {{IPA|/eo/}}). In [[Upper German]] varieties, it also becomes {{IPA|/iu/}} before labials and velars.
* {{IPA|/θ/}} fortifies to {{IPA|/d/}} in all German dialects.
* Initial {{IPA|/w/}} and {{IPA|/h/}} before another consonant are dropped.

